<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<!--
  Sample Past Exam 1, with solutions.

  Transcribed from the instructor's annotated solutions PDF of the Fall
  2025 first exam (Exam_1-MATH14-Fall2025-Solutions.pdf): the five exam
  questions and the handwritten solutions, followed by question 3 of the
  Spring 2025 second exam (MATH_14_Exam__2_F2025-Solutions.pdf) as a sixth
  question.  Point values are deliberately left out.  Modeled on the MATH 13
  instructor book's sample-past-exam-1.ptx: one exercise per question, with
  a task per part and a solution under each.  Included from main.ptx right after Review Problems #1, in
  this book and in the completed instructor book.  The xml:id keeps the
  "sec-exercises-" prefix so assets/unnumbered-chapter.css hides its number
  like the rest of the chapter, and xsl/custom-html.xsl leaves the
  worksheet out of its born-visible rule, so the solutions sit behind
  knowls as on the MATH 13 sample exam page.

  Two notes from typesetting:
  - Question 3: the handwritten antiderivative had the factor (minus 5/2)
    distributed over cos(sin t) as well; it belongs only to t minus
    (1/2) sin 2t.  The cos(sin t) term vanishes over a full period either
    way, so the answer, minus 5 pi, is unchanged.  Set correctly here.
  - Question 1 calls the curve a helix, as the exam did.  Its points all
    lie at distance 2 sqrt 2 from the origin in the plane y = minus x, so it
    is in fact a circle; the statement is left as it was given.
-->
<worksheet xml:id="sec-exercises-sample-past-exam-1" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Sample Past Exam 1</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      A sample past exam, with worked solutions. It was a closed-book
      exam: no aids of one's own (formula sheets, notes, phones,
      tablets, smart watches or similar) were permitted, and students were
      asked to write their work in a neat and organized format and to fully
      justify every step. Try the exam under the same conditions before you
      look at the solutions. The topics it covers, with links to the notes:
    </p>
    <p>
      <ul>
        <li><p><xref ref="sec-parametrization" text="title"/></p></li>
        <li><p><xref ref="sec-arc-length" text="title"/></p></li>
        <li><p><xref ref="sec-line-integrals" text="title"/></p></li>
        <li><p><xref ref="sec-vector-fields" text="title"/></p></li>
        <li><p>Path Independence, Conservative Fields, and Potential Functions
          <!-- plain text until that section is posted: <xref ref="section-conservative-fields"/> --></p></li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ===================== Question 1 ===================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="spe1-q1">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        The helix starting from <m>(2, -2, 0)</m> is parametrized as
        <me>
          \mathbf{r}(t) = \left\langle 2\cos(t),\, -2\cos(t),\, 2\sqrt{2}\sin(t) \right\rangle .
        </me>
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="spe1-q1-a">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find the arc length <m>s(t)</m> of the helix, measured from
          <m>(2, -2, 0)</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The velocity is
          <me>
            \mathbf{v}(t) = \left\langle -2\sin t,\, 2\sin t,\, 2\sqrt{2}\cos t \right\rangle,
          </me>
          so the speed is
          <me>
            \lVert \mathbf{v}(t) \rVert
            = \sqrt{4\sin^2 t + 4\sin^2 t + 8\cos^2 t}
            = \sqrt{8} = 2\sqrt{2}.
          </me>
          The starting point <m>(2,-2,0)</m> is <m>\mathbf{r}(0)</m>, so
          <me>
            s(t) = \int_0^t \lVert \mathbf{v}(\tau) \rVert \, d\tau
            = \int_0^t 2\sqrt{2}\, d\tau = 2\sqrt{2}\, t .
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="spe1-q1-b">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Reparametrize the helix with respect to arc length.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Solving <m>s = 2\sqrt{2}\, t</m> for <m>t</m> gives
          <m>t = \dfrac{s}{2\sqrt{2}}</m>, hence
          <md>
            <mrow>\mathbf{r}(t(s)) = \Bigl\langle\,
              2\cos\!\Bigl(\tfrac{s}{2\sqrt{2}}\Bigr),\;
              -2\cos\!\Bigl(\tfrac{s}{2\sqrt{2}}\Bigr),</mrow>
            <mrow>\qquad\qquad
              2\sqrt{2}\,\sin\!\Bigl(\tfrac{s}{2\sqrt{2}}\Bigr)
              \Bigr\rangle .</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ===================== Question 2 ===================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="spe1-q2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Find the total mass of a wire in the shape of the parabola
        <m>y = x^2</m> for <m>\sqrt{2} \le x \le \sqrt{6}</m>, where
        <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> are measured in centimeters. Assume that the
        mass density of the wire is given by <m>\rho(x,y) = y/x</m> in
        grams per centimeter.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Parametrize the wire by <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle t, t^2 \rangle</m>,
        <m>\sqrt{2} \le t \le \sqrt{6}</m>. Along it the density is
        <me>
          \rho\bigl(x(t), y(t)\bigr) = \frac{t^2}{t} = t,
        </me>
        and <m>\mathbf{v}(t) = \langle 1, 2t \rangle</m>, so
        <m>\lVert \mathbf{v}(t) \rVert = \sqrt{1 + 4t^2}</m>. Therefore
        <md>
          <mrow>M \amp = \int_C \rho(x,y)\, ds
            = \int_{\sqrt{2}}^{\sqrt{6}} t\sqrt{1 + 4t^2}\, dt</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \frac{1}{8} \int_{\sqrt{2}}^{\sqrt{6}} 8t\sqrt{1 + 4t^2}\, dt</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \frac{1}{8}\cdot\frac{2}{3}
            \left(1 + 4t^2\right)^{3/2} \Bigg|_{\sqrt{2}}^{\sqrt{6}}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \frac{1}{12}\left(5^3 - 3^3\right)
            = \frac{98}{12},</mrow>
        </md>
        so the mass is <m>M = \dfrac{98}{12} = \dfrac{49}{6}</m> grams.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ===================== Question 3 ===================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="spe1-q3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Evaluate the line integral
        <m>\displaystyle\int_C 5y\, dx - \sin(y)\, dy</m>, where <m>C</m>
        is the counter-clockwise oriented unit circle.
      </p>
    </statement>
    <solution>
      <p>
        Take <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle \cos t, \sin t \rangle</m>,
        <m>0 \le t \le 2\pi</m>, so that
        <m>\mathbf{v}(t) = \langle -\sin t, \cos t \rangle</m>. The field is
        <m>\mathbf{F} = \langle 5y, -\sin(y) \rangle</m>, which along
        <m>C</m> is
        <m>\mathbf{F} = \langle 5\sin t, -\sin(\sin t) \rangle</m>, and
        <md>
          <mrow>\mathbf{F} \cdot \mathbf{v}
            \amp = -5\sin^2 t - \cos t \, \sin(\sin t)</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = -\frac{5}{2}\bigl(1 - \cos(2t)\bigr) - \cos t \, \sin(\sin t).</mrow>
        </md>
        Hence
        <md>
          <mrow>\int_C 5y\, dx \amp - \sin(y)\, dy</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \int_{0}^{2\pi} \Bigl[ -\tfrac{5}{2}\bigl(1 - \cos(2t)\bigr)
              - \cos t \, \sin(\sin t) \Bigr] dt</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = \Bigl[ -\tfrac{5}{2}\bigl(t - \tfrac{1}{2}\sin(2t)\bigr)
              + \cos(\sin t) \Bigr]_0^{2\pi}</mrow>
          <mrow>\amp = -\frac{5}{2}\,(2\pi) = -5\pi .</mrow>
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ===================== Question 4 ===================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="spe1-q4">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Find a parametrization of the curve of intersection of the
        following surfaces.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="spe1-q4-a">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>z = 3x + y</m> and <m>y = 5x^2</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          Let <m>x = t</m>. Then <m>y = 5t^2</m> and
          <m>z = 3t + 5t^2</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>x(t) \amp = t,</mrow>
            <mrow>y(t) \amp = 5t^2,</mrow>
            <mrow>z(t) \amp = 3t + 5t^2, \qquad t \in \mathbb{R}.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="spe1-q4-b">
      <statement>
        <p>
          <m>x^2 + y^2 = 9</m> and <m>z = 2x + y</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The cylinder gives <m>x = 3\cos t</m>, <m>y = 3\sin t</m>, and
          then <m>z = 2x + y = 6\cos t + 3\sin t</m>:
          <md>
            <mrow>x(t) \amp = 3\cos t,</mrow>
            <mrow>y(t) \amp = 3\sin t,</mrow>
            <mrow>z(t) \amp = 6\cos t + 3\sin t, \qquad 0 \le t \le 2\pi.</mrow>
          </md>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ===================== Question 5 ===================== -->
  <exercise xml:id="spe1-q5">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        <alert>True or False? Clearly justify your answer.</alert> Note
        that correct answers with incorrect or unclear justifications will
        not earn credit.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="spe1-q5-a">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Consider the line integral <m>\displaystyle\int_C f\, ds</m>,
          where <m>f</m> is a scalar function. Reversing the direction in
          which the integral is taken along a curve changes the sign of
          the line integral,
          <m>\displaystyle\int_{-C} f\, ds = -\int_C f\, ds</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <alert>False.</alert> Let <m>C</m> be
          <m>\mathbf{r}(t) = \langle x(t), y(t) \rangle</m>,
          <m>a \le t \le b</m>. Then <m>-C</m> is traced by
          <m>\mathbf{r}(a+b-t) = \langle x(a+b-t), y(a+b-t) \rangle</m>,
          <m>a \le t \le b</m>, and with the substitution
          <m>u = a+b-t</m>, <m>du = -dt</m>,
          <md>
            <mrow>\int_{-C} f\, ds
              \amp = \int_{a}^{b} f\bigl(\mathbf{r}(a+b-t)\bigr)\,
                \lVert \mathbf{v}(a+b-t) \rVert\, dt</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \int_{u=b}^{u=a} f\bigl(\mathbf{r}(u)\bigr)\,
                \lVert \mathbf{v}(u) \rVert\, (-du)</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = -\int_{u=b}^{u=a} f\bigl(\mathbf{r}(u)\bigr)\,
                \lVert \mathbf{v}(u) \rVert\, du</mrow>
            <mrow>\amp = \int_{u=a}^{u=b} f\bigl(\mathbf{r}(u)\bigr)\,
                \lVert \mathbf{v}(u) \rVert\, du
              = \int_C f\, ds .</mrow>
          </md>
          The line integral of a scalar function does not change sign when
          the direction is reversed.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="spe1-q5-b">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>f(x,y,z) = \cos\left(x^2 + y^2 + z^2\right) - 2</m> and
          <m>C</m> is a smooth curve from point <m>A</m> to point <m>B</m>,
          then <m>\displaystyle\int_C f(x,y,z)\, ds \gt 0</m>.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <alert>False.</alert> Since the cosine is at most <m>1</m>,
          <m>f(x,y,z) \le -1</m> everywhere, and therefore
          <me>
            \int_C f(x,y,z)\, ds \lt 0 .
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

  <!-- ===================== Question 6 ===================== -->
  <!-- Question 3 of the Spring 2025 Exam 2. -->
  <figure xml:id="fig-spe1-field">
    <caption>
      The vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> and the circle <m>C</m> of
      <xref ref="spe1-q6"/>. Every field vector is horizontal and has the
      same length: they point right above the <m>x</m>-axis and left
      below it.
    </caption>
    <image width="60%">
      <shortdescription>
      A horizontal field, rightward above the x-axis and leftward below it, with a circle centered at the origin.
      </shortdescription>
      <prefigure xmlns="https://prefigure.org" label="prefigure-spe1-field">
        <diagram dimensions="(360,360)" margins="10">
          <coordinates bbox="(-4.2,-4.2,4.2,4.2)">
            <axes at="sf-axes" xlabel="x" ylabel="y" decorations="no" arrows="1"/>
            <repeat at="sf-upper" parameter="i=-3..3">
              <repeat parameter="j=0..3">
                <vector at="sf-u-((i))-((j))" v="(0.55,0)"
                    tail="(i-0.275, 0.5+j)" stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
              </repeat>
            </repeat>
            <repeat at="sf-lower" parameter="i=-3..3">
              <repeat parameter="j=0..3">
                <vector at="sf-l-((i))-((j))" v="(-0.55,0)"
                    tail="(i+0.275, -0.5-j)" stroke="#56B4E9" thickness="2"/>
              </repeat>
            </repeat>
            <circle at="sf-C" center="(0,0)" radius="2.5" fill="none"
                stroke="black" thickness="2"/>
            <label at="sf-Clabel" anchor="(1.95,1.95)" alignment="ne"><m>C</m></label>
            <label at="sf-Olabel" anchor="(-0.15,-0.15)" alignment="sw"><m>O</m></label>
          </coordinates>
          <annotations>
            <annotation ref="figure" text="A grid of light blue horizontal field vectors of equal length. Above the x axis they all point to the right; below it they all point to the left. A black circle C is centered at the origin O and cuts through the grid, so that along its upper half the field points right and along its lower half the field points left.">
              <annotation ref="sf-upper" text="The field vectors above the x axis, all pointing to the right."/>
              <annotation ref="sf-lower" text="The field vectors below the x axis, all pointing to the left."/>
              <annotation ref="sf-C" text="The circle C, centered at the origin."/>
            </annotation>
          </annotations>
        </diagram>
      </prefigure>
    </image>
  </figure>

  <exercise xml:id="spe1-q6">
    <introduction>
      <p>
        Let <m>C</m> be a circle centered at the origin. Also, assume that
        the vector field <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is the vector field which is
        graphed in <xref ref="fig-spe1-field"/>.
      </p>
    </introduction>

    <task xml:id="spe1-q6-a">
      <statement>
        <p>
          If <m>C</m> is traced counterclockwise, is
          <m>\displaystyle\int_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}</m> positive,
          negative, or zero? Explain.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <alert>Negative.</alert> Along the upper half of <m>C</m> the
          counterclockwise unit tangent <m>\hat{\mathbf{T}}</m> points
          leftward while <m>\mathbf{F}</m> points right; along the lower
          half <m>\hat{\mathbf{T}}</m> points rightward while
          <m>\mathbf{F}</m> points left. So the angle <m>\theta</m> between
          <m>\hat{\mathbf{T}}</m> and <m>\mathbf{F}</m> is obtuse everywhere
          along <m>C</m>, which means
          <m>\mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{T}} \lt 0</m>, and hence
          <me>
            \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r}
            = \oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{T}}\, ds \lt 0 .
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>

    <task xml:id="spe1-q6-b">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Is <m>\mathbf{F}</m> conservative? Explain.
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          <alert>No.</alert> If <m>\mathbf{F}</m> were conservative, we
          would have <m>\displaystyle\oint_C \mathbf{F} \cdot d\mathbf{r} = 0</m>
          for any loop <m>C</m>, and part (a) found a loop around which the
          circulation is not zero.
        </p>
      </solution>
    </task>
  </exercise>

</worksheet>
